LiDAR (/scan)
     ↓
Localization (AMCL)
     ↓
Costmap
     ↓
Planner
     ↓
Controller
     ↓
/cmd_vel → Robot moves

All of this is handled by:

    📦 Navigation2 (Nav2)

🧩 STEP 1 — Install Nav2 (if not installed)

sudo apt install ros-humble-nav2-bringup

🧩 STEP 2 — Launch TurtleBot3 Simulation

Terminal 1:

export TURTLEBOT3_MODEL=burger
ros2 launch turtlebot3_gazebo turtlebot3_world.launch.py

Wait until robot fully loads.
🧩 STEP 3 — Launch Navigation Stack

Terminal 2:

export TURTLEBOT3_MODEL=burger
ros2 launch turtlebot3_navigation2 navigation2.launch.py use_sim_time:=True

What this launches:

    AMCL (localization)

    Planner

    Controller

    Behavior Tree Navigator

    Costmaps

🧩 STEP 4 — Open RViz

If RViz doesn’t open automatically:

ros2 run rviz2 rviz2

Make sure:

    Fixed Frame = map

    You see:

        Map

        Laser scan

        Robot model

        Costmap

🧩 STEP 5 — Set Initial Pose (VERY IMPORTANT)

In RViz:

    Click “2D Pose Estimate”

    Click on robot location

    Drag to set orientation

This initializes localization.
🧩 STEP 6 — Send Goal

In RViz:

    Click “Nav2 Goal”

    Click somewhere in the map

🎉 Robot should:

    Plan path

    Avoid obstacles using LiDAR

    Move to goal

🧠 What’s Actually Using LiDAR?

LiDAR (/scan) is used by:

    AMCL → localization

    Local costmap → obstacle avoidance

    Controller → safe velocity commands

🔥 If Robot Doesn’t Move

Check:

ros2 topic echo /scan

If data is coming → LiDAR is working.

Check Nav2 lifecycle:

ros2 lifecycle nodes

All should be ACTIVE.
